    "=0.0009765625\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus the root is accurate to about three decimal places.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Charecterstics\n",
    "\n",
    "### Advantages\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Simple: Easy to understand and implement.\n",
    "2. Guaranteed Convergence  \n",
    "    If:\n",
    "    * f(x) is continuous\n",
    "    * f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs  \n",
    "    the method will always converge to a root.\n",
    "3. Stable: Less sensitive to numerical errors than many other methods.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Disadvantages\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Slow Convergence\n",
    "    * The interval only halves each iteration.\n",
    "    * Methods like Newton-Raphson usually converge much faster.\n",
    "2. Requires Bracketing: Need initial points satisfying\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f(a)f(b)<0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Cannot Find Some Roots Easily: If the function does not change sign around a root (for example, a repeated root), the method may fail.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Key Points to Remember\n",
    "\n",
    "* Bisection Method is a **bracketing method**.\n",
    "* It works only when the function changes sign in the chosen interval.\n",
    "* Each iteration halves the interval containing the root.\n",
    "* Convergence is guaranteed for continuous functions with opposite signs at the endpoints.\n",
    "* It is slower than methods such as Newton-Raphson, but it is very reliable and widely used as a foundational numerical root-finding technique.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Newton Raphson Method\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Newton–Raphson Method** (also called **Newton's Method**) is one of the most powerful and widely used numerical techniques for finding the roots of nonlinear equations. The method starts with an initial guess and repeatedly improves that guess until it gets very close to the actual root.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Basic Idea\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose we want to find a root of:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f(x)=0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and we have an initial approximation $x_0$.\n",
    "\n",
    "At the point $(x_0,f(x_0))$, draw the **tangent line** to the curve. The point where this tangent line intersects the x-axis gives a better approximation of the root. This process is repeated until the approximation converges.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Geometric Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "For a function $f(x)$:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Choose an initial guess $x_0$.\n",
    "2. Draw the tangent at $(x_0,f(x_0))$.\n",
    "3. Find where the tangent crosses the x-axis.\n",
    "4. Use this x-intercept as the next approximation.\n",
    "5. Repeat.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the tangent often points toward the root, convergence is usually very fast.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Derivation of the Formula\n",
    "\n",
    "The equation of the tangent line at $x_n$ is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y-f(x_n)=f'(x_n)(x_{n+1}-x_n)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "At the x-axis:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y=0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Substituting:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "-f(x_n)=f'(x_n)(x_{n+1}-x_n)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Solving for $x_{n+1}$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_{n+1}=x_n-\\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the **Newton–Raphson iteration formula**.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Algorithm\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step 1\n",
    "\n",
    "Choose an initial guess:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step 2\n",
    "\n",
    "Compute:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f(x_0)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f'(x_0)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step 3\n",
    "\n",
    "Calculate:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_1=x_0-\\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step 4\n",
    "\n",
    "Repeat:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_{n+1}=x_n-\\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "until the desired accuracy is achieved.\n"
