    "  * Matrix Inversion\n",
    "\n",
    "### Disadvantages\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Cannot Handle Zero Pivots: Fails when\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "a_{kk}=0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Numerical Instability: Very small pivots can cause large round-off errors.\n",
    "3. Less Reliable: Modern numerical software rarely uses pure naive elimination; it typically uses pivoting strategies.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Difference Between Naive Gaussian Elimination and Gaussian Elimination with Pivoting\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature             | Naive Elimination | Pivoted Elimination  |\n",
    "| ------------------- | ----------------- | -------------------- |\n",
    "| Row Swapping        | No                | Yes                  |\n",
    "| Handles Zero Pivot  | No                | Yes                  |\n",
    "| Numerical Stability | Lower             | Higher               |\n",
    "| Practical Use       | Educational       | Real-world computing |\n",
    "| Reliability         | Moderate          | High                 |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Naive Gaussian Elimination Method** solves linear systems by:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Eliminating variables through **forward elimination**.\n",
    "2. Solving the resulting upper triangular system using **back substitution**.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the system\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "2x+y-z &= 8\\\\\n",
    "-3x-y+2z &= -11\\\\\n",
    "-2x+y+2z &= -3\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "the method produces:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{x=2,;y=3,;z=-1}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The method is simple and educationally important, but because it does **not use pivoting**, it can fail for zero pivots and may suffer from numerical instability. For practical scientific computing, **Gaussian Elimination with Partial Pivoting** is generally preferred.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Partial Pivoting Method\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Partial Pivoting Method** is an improved version of **Naive Gaussian Elimination** used to solve systems of linear equations.\n",
    "\n",
    "Its main purpose is to:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Avoid division by zero.\n",
    "* Reduce round-off errors.\n",
    "* Improve numerical stability.\n",
    "\n",
    "Before each elimination step, the method chooses the **largest absolute value** in the current column as the pivot and swaps rows if necessary.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why Partial Pivoting Is Needed\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider the system:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "0x + 2y &= 4\\\\\n",
    "x + y &= 3\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Augmented matrix:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\left[\n",
    "\\begin{array}{cc|c}\n",
    "0 & 2 & 4\\\\\n",
    "1 & 1 & 3\n",
    "\\end{array}\n",
    "\\right]\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "In Naive Gaussian Elimination, the first pivot is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "a_{11}=0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The multiplier would be:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "m_{21}\n",
    "=\\frac{1}{0}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "which is impossible.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Solution\n",
    "\n",
    "Swap the two rows:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Now the pivot becomes 1 and elimination can proceed.\n",
    "\n",
    "This row-swapping process is called **pivoting**.\n",
    "\n",
    "## What Is Partial Pivoting?\n",
    "\n",
    "At each elimination step:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Look down the current pivot column.\n",
    "2. Find the element with the largest absolute value.\n",
    "3. Swap that row with the current pivot row.\n",
    "4. Continue elimination normally.\n",
    "\n",
    "This ensures that the pivot is as large as possible.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Mathematical Idea\n",
    "\n",
    "For column (k), select row (p) such that:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "|a_{pk}|\n",
    "=\\max_{i\\ge k}|a_{ik}|\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then exchange:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "R_k\n",
    "\\leftrightarrow\n",
    "R_p\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "After swapping, elimination proceeds exactly as in Gaussian Elimination.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Algorithm\n",
    "\n",
    "For each column (k):\n",
    "\n",
    "### Find Largest Pivot\n",
    "\n",
    "Search:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "|a_{kk}|,;|a_{k+1,k}|,\\ldots,|a_{nk}|\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Choose the largest.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Swap Rows\n",
    "\n",
    "Move the row containing the largest element to the pivot position.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Eliminate Entries Below Pivot\n",
    "\n",
    "Compute multiplier:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "m_{ik}\n",
    "=\\frac{a_{ik}}{a_{kk}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Perform:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "R_i\n",
    "=R_i-m_{ik}R_k\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Repeat\n",
    "\n",
    "Continue for remaining columns.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Back Substitution\n",
    "\n",
    "Solve the upper triangular system."
