    "✓\n",
    "\n",
    "### Comparison: Naive vs Partial Pivoting\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature             | Naive Gaussian Elimination | Partial Pivoting |\n",
    "| ------------------- | -------------------------- | ---------------- |\n",
    "| Row Swapping        | No                         | Yes              |\n",
    "| Handles Zero Pivot  | No                         | Yes              |\n",
    "| Numerical Stability | Lower                      | Higher           |\n",
    "| Round-off Error     | Larger                     | Smaller          |\n",
    "| Practical Use       | Rare                       | Very Common      |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Advantages of Partial Pivoting\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Prevents Division by Zero: If a pivot is zero, row swapping provides a nonzero pivot.\n",
    "2. Improves Accuracy: Using the largest pivot reduces round-off errors.\n",
    "3. More Stable: Works reliably for a wider range of systems.\n",
    "4. Widely Used: Most numerical software libraries use Gaussian Elimination with Partial Pivoting.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Disadvantages\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Extra Computation: Must search for the largest pivot in each column.\n",
    "2. Slightly More Complex: Requires row exchanges and bookkeeping.\n",
    "3. Not Perfect: For some extremely ill-conditioned matrices, even partial pivoting may not be sufficient (full pivoting may be preferred).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Partial Pivoting Method** is an enhanced version of Gaussian Elimination that improves numerical stability by selecting the largest available pivot in each column and swapping rows when necessary.\n",
    "\n",
    "The process consists of:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Pivot selection** (largest absolute value in the column).\n",
    "2. **Row swapping**.\n",
    "3. **Forward elimination**.\n",
    "4. **Back substitution**.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the example system\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "2x+y-z &= 8\\\\\n",
    "-3x-y+2z &= -11\\\\\n",
    "-2x+y+2z &= -3\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "the method gives:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{x=2,\\quad y=3,\\quad z=-1}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "while providing better numerical stability than Naive Gaussian Elimination.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Direct Methods of Interpolation\n",
    "\n",
    "**Interpolation** is a numerical technique used to estimate the value of a function at a point lying **between known data points**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose we know the values:\n",
    "\n",
    "| x | y=f(x) |\n",
    "| - | ------ |\n",
    "| 1 | 2      |\n",
    "| 2 | 5      |\n",
    "| 3 | 10     |\n",
    "\n",
    "and want to estimate f(2.5).\n",
    "\n",
    "Interpolation constructs a function (usually a polynomial) that passes through the known points and then uses that function to estimate the unknown value.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What Are Direct Methods of Interpolation?**\n",
    "\n",
    "**Direct interpolation methods** determine the interpolating polynomial directly from the given data points without using finite difference tables.\n",
    "\n",
    "The most common direct methods are:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Linear Interpolation**\n",
    "2. **Lagrange Interpolation**\n",
    "3. **Newton's Divided Difference Interpolation**\n",
    "\n",
    "These methods construct an explicit interpolation formula from the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Linear Interpolation\n",
    "\n",
    "Linear interpolation assumes that the curve between two adjacent points is approximately a straight line.\n",
    "\n",
    "Given two points:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "(x_0,y_0)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "(x_1,y_1)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "the interpolating equation is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y=y_0+\\frac{y_1-y_0}{x_1-x_0}(x-x_0)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Estimate y at x=2.5.\n",
    "\n",
    "Given:\n",
    "\n",
    "| x | y  |\n",
    "| - | -- |\n",
    "| 2 | 5  |\n",
    "| 3 | 10 |\n",
    "\n",
    "Here:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_0=2,\\quad y_0=5\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_1=3,\\quad y_1=10\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Using the formula:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y =5+\\frac{10-5}{3-2}(2.5-2)\n",
    "=7.5\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Result\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{f(2.5)\\approx7.5}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Advantages\n",
    "\n",
    "* Very simple.\n",
    "* Fast computation.\n",
    "* Useful when only two points are available.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Disadvantages\n",
    "\n",
    "* Accuracy is limited.\n",
    "* Assumes a straight-line relationship.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Lagrange Interpolation\n",
    "\n",
    "Lagrange interpolation constructs a single polynomial passing through all given data points.\n",
    "\n",
    "If there are (n+1) data points:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "(x_0,y_0),\n",
    "(x_1,y_1),\n",
    "\\ldots,\n",
    "(x_n,y_n)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "then the interpolation polynomial is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "P(x)\n",
    "=\\sum_{i=0}^{n}\n",
    "y_iL_i(x)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "L_i(x)\n",
    "=\\prod_{j=0,j\\neq i}^{n}\n",
    "\\frac{x-x_j}{x_i-x_j}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Given:\n",
    "\n",
    "| x | y |\n",
    "| - | - |\n",
    "| 1 | 1 |\n",
    "| 2 | 4 |\n",
    "| 3 | 9 |\n",
    "\n",
    "Find f(2.5).\n",
    "\n",
    "Notice:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y=x^2\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "but we'll use Lagrange interpolation.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Compute Basis Polynomials\n",
    "\n",
    "For $x_0=1$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "L_0(x)\n",
    "=\\frac{(x-2)(x-3)}\n",
    "{(1-2)(1-3)}\n",
    "=\\frac{(x-2)(x-3)}{2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For $x_1=2$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "L_1(x)\n",
    "=\\frac{(x-1)(x-3)}\n",
    "{(2-1)(2-3)}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For $x_2=3$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "L_2(x)\n",
    "=\\frac{(x-1)(x-2)}\n",
    "{(3-1)(3-2)}\n",
    "=\\frac{(x-1)(x-2)}{2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Construct Polynomial\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "P(x)\n",
    "=1L_0(x)\n",
    "+\n",
    "4L_1(x)\n",
    "+\n",
    "9L_2(x)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Substituting $x=2.5$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "P(2.5)\n",
    "=6.25\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Result\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{f(2.5)=6.25}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "which matches the exact value of $2.5^2$.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Advantages\n",
    "\n",
    "* Works for unequal spacing.\n",
    "* Easy to understand.\n",
    "* No difference table required.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Disadvantages\n",
    "\n",
    "* Computation becomes lengthy for many points.\n",
    "* Entire polynomial must be recomputed when a new point is added."
   ]
