    "## Charecterstics\n",
    "\n",
    "### Comparison of Direct Interpolation Methods\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature                | Linear   | Lagrange | Newton Divided Difference |\n",
    "| ---------------------- | -------- | -------- | ------------------------- |\n",
    "| Number of Points       | 2        | Many     | Many                      |\n",
    "| Accuracy               | Low      | High     | High                      |\n",
    "| Unequal Spacing        | Yes      | Yes      | Yes                       |\n",
    "| Easy to Add New Points | No       | No       | Yes                       |\n",
    "| Complexity             | Very Low | Medium   | Medium                    |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Applications of Interpolation\n",
    "\n",
    "Interpolation is widely used in:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Engineering design\n",
    "* Scientific experiments\n",
    "* Weather forecasting\n",
    "* Computer graphics\n",
    "* Signal processing\n",
    "* Data analysis\n",
    "* Machine learning\n",
    "* Financial modeling\n",
    "\n",
    "### Example Applications\n",
    "\n",
    "* Estimating temperature between measured times.\n",
    "* Estimating population between census years.\n",
    "* Filling missing sensor data.\n",
    "* Generating smooth curves in computer graphics.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Difference Between Interpolation and Extrapolation\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Interpolation\n",
    "\n",
    "Estimate within the known data range.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example: Known data between (x=1) and (x=5), estimate at (x=3).\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Extrapolation\n",
    "\n",
    "Estimate outside the known data range.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Known data up to (x=5), estimate at (x=8).\n",
    "* Extrapolation is generally less reliable.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "**Direct Methods of Interpolation** construct an interpolating function directly from known data points to estimate unknown values.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main direct methods are:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Linear Interpolation** – uses a straight line between two points.\n",
    "2. **Lagrange Interpolation** – constructs a polynomial using basis functions.\n",
    "3. **Newton's Divided Difference Interpolation** – builds a polynomial incrementally using divided differences.\n",
    "\n",
    "These methods are fundamental tools in numerical analysis because they allow us to estimate unknown values accurately from discrete data without knowing the original function explicitly.\n",
    "\n",
    "# LU Decomposition Method\n",
    "\n",
    "**LU Decomposition** (or **LU Factorization**) is a numerical method used to solve a system of linear equations by decomposing a matrix into two simpler matrices:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "A = LU\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* (L) = **Lower Triangular Matrix**\n",
    "* (U) = **Upper Triangular Matrix**\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of solving:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "AX=B\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "directly, we solve two simpler systems:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "LY=B\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and then\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "UX=Y\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This method is particularly useful when the same coefficient matrix (A) is used with multiple different right-hand-side vectors (B).\n",
    "\n",
    "## What Are Lower and Upper Triangular Matrices\n",
    "\n",
    "### Lower Triangular Matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "All elements above the main diagonal are zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "L=\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
    "1 & 0 & 0\\\\\n",
    "l_{21} & 1 & 0\\\\\n",
    "l_{31} & l_{32} & 1\n",
    "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Upper Triangular Matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "All elements below the main diagonal are zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "U=\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
    "u_{11} & u_{12} & u_{13}\\\\\n",
    "0 & u_{22} & u_{23}\\\\\n",
    "0 & 0 & u_{33}\n",
    "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "## Basic Idea\n",
    "\n",
    "Given:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "AX=B\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Factorize:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "A=LU\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "LUX=B\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Let:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "UX=Y\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "LY=B\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Now solve:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. (LY=B) using **forward substitution**\n",
    "2. (UX=Y) using **back substitution**\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why LU Decomposition\n",
    "\n",
    "Gaussian Elimination transforms a matrix into upper triangular form. LU Decomposition stores the elimination process in the lower triangular matrix (L).\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus:\n",
    "\n",
    "* (U) contains the final upper triangular matrix.\n",
    "* (L) contains the multipliers used during elimination.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# Doolittle's LU Decomposition\n",
    "\n",
    "The most common form is **Doolittle's Method**, where:\n",
    "\n",
    "[\n",
    "L\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "has ones on the diagonal.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n"
